 when $a=2.$" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356365 13194139535587 true] [17592186356364 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535587 true] [17592186356364 1041 "field-1" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356364 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356365 13194139535587 true] [17592186356364 1110 #uuid "e972cce8-25e1-546f-8537-e9871879e422" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356365 1034 "2" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356365 1111 #uuid "32f25e40-33ce-5db2-a546-87db04b5cb91" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356366 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356367 13194139535587 true] [17592186356366 1089 "q-316023" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356366 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535587 true] [17592186356366 1100 "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0),$ where $b$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535587 true] [17592186356366 1113 #uuid "16182e1a-298f-50f8-bdb7-e58926f8fcb9" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356366 1145 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & ={(b-3)}^{2}+{(2b-0)}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b)^{2} \\\\  & =({b}^{2}-6b+9)+(4{b}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-6b+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(b)=10b-6.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-6 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\,(\\frac{3}{5})=10>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(b)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $b=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-6(\\frac{3}{5})+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{5}-\\frac{18}{5}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0)$ is $\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356368 13194139535587 true] [17592186356367 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535587 true] [17592186356367 1041 "field-1" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356367 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356368 13194139535587 true] [17592186356367 1110 #uuid "b54c0399-4ce0-50aa-bf89-eeb7acec2d87" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356368 1034 "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356368 1111 #uuid "a0793005-6fc5-585e-9280-e7e077bb728b" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356369 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356370 13194139535587 true] [17592186356369 1089 "q-344629" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356369 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535587 true] [17592186356369 1100 "Find the value of $b$ that minimizes the distance between the points $(5b+3,\\,b-1)$ and $(4,\\,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356369 1113 #uuid "3d93bc12-fef0-5a49-ab5f-dd63888aaf92" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356369 1145 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & =((5b+3)-4)^{2}+((b-1)-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(5b-1)^{2}+(b-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(25{b}^{2}-10b+1)+({b}^{2}-6b+9) \\\\  & =26{b}^{2}-16b+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S$, we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(b)=52b-16.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 52b-16 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{4}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(b)=52>0.$\n\nTherefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{4}{13}.$" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356371 13194139535587 true] [17592186356370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356372 13194139535587 true] [17592186356370 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535587 true] [17592186356370 1041 "field-1" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356370 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356371 13194139535587 true] [17592186356370 1110 #uuid "14fce101-859e-5eb3-83d8-7e00861d2288" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356371 1034 "\\frac{4}{13}" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356371 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356371 1111 #uuid "b41e7542-681e-5a23-906c-d6cdd54266f9" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356372 1034 "0" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356372 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356372 1111 #uuid "47a24c95-d598-5016-9a5d-726fdc39f6a7" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356373 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356374 13194139535587 true] [17592186356373 1089 "q-344635" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356373 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535587 true] [17592186356373 1100 "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(4x,\\,-x)$ and $(5,\\,-1),$ where $x$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535587 true] [17592186356373 1113 #uuid "43031d2f-e8dc-5b6a-bf01-0a97a6d30271" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356373 1145 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(x)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={((4x)-5)}^{2}+{((-x)-(-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(4x-5)^{2}+(-x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(16{x}^{2}-40x+25)+({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =17{x}^{2}-42x+26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=34x-42.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 34x-42 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{21}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\,(\\frac{21}{17})=34>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x=\\frac{21}{17}.$ \n\nSince $S(x)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{21}{17}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{21}{17})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{21}{17})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{(\\frac{21}{17})}^{2}-42(\\frac{21}{17})+26} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{441}{17}-\\frac{882}{17}+26} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(4x,\\,-x)$ and $(5,\\,-1)$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}.$" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356375 13194139535587 true] [17592186356374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356376 13194139535587 true] [17592186356374 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535587 true] [17592186356374 1041 "field-1" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356374 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356376 13194139535587 true] [17592186356374 1110 #uuid "c27f1987-f21f-5986-a0ec-c3724bd095a6" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356375 1034 "0" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356375 1111 #uuid "920fae60-9add-519a-89d8-51e6f144ae42" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356376 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356376 1111 #uuid "fb00d0dd-223e-592b-8d15-1d9a729d55a1" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356377 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356378 13194139535587 true] [17592186356377 1089 "q-344637" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356377 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535587 true] [17592186356377 1100 "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(-4x,\\,2x-3)$ and $(1,\\,5),$ where $x$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535587 true] [17592186356377 1113 #uuid "ad6188c5-84a1-5ea4-91d6-e360ef7fa379" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356377 1145 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(x)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={((-4x)-1)}^{2}+{((2x-3)-5)}^{2} \\\\  & =(-4x-1)^{2}+(2x-8)^{2} \\\\  & =16{x}^{2}+8x+1+4{x}^{2}-32x+64 \\\\  & =20{x}^{2}-24x+65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=40x-24.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 40x-24 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{3}{5})=40>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(x)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-24(\\frac{3}{5})+65} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{289}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(-4x,\\,2x-3)$ and $(1,\\,5)$ is $\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356379 13194139535587 true] [17592186356378 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535587 true] [17592186356378 1041 "field-1" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356378 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356379 13194139535587 true] [17592186356378 1110 #uuid "9bc19be2-9b35-5071-8a7a-c34e536c9a4e" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356379 1034 "\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356379 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356379 1111 #uuid "2597a3ed-ff01-54c6-ba46-7270e5dc99ce" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356380 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356381 13194139535587 true] [17592186356380 1089 "q-344638" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356380 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535587 true] [17592186356380 1100 "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(2a-1,\\,a)$ and $(3,\\,-4),$ where $a$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535587 true] [17592186356380 1113 #uuid "09b5873b-e55f-5686-8fc8-75564948e205" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356380 1145 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={((2a-1)-3)}^{2}+{(a-(-4))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2a-4)^{2}+(a+4)^{2} \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}-16a+16+{a}^{2}+8a+16 \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-8a+32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(a)=10a-8.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-8 & =0 \\\\ a & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{4}{5})=10>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=\\frac{4}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $a=\\frac{4}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{4}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}-8(\\frac{4}{5})+32} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{144}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(2a-1,\\,a)$ and $(3,\\,-4)$ is $\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356382 13194139535587 true] [17592186356381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356383 13194139535587 true] [17592186356381 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535587 true] [17592186356381 1041 "field-1" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356381 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356382 13194139535587 true] [17592186356381 1110 #uuid "7459ed4a-69d0-57de-8c5b-93c18d5b32bb" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356382 1034 "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356382 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356382 1111 #uuid "bc00d615-bc93-55ec-b924-a2c2b906f3c2" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356383 1034 "0" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356383 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356383 1111 #uuid "35544a26-08d5-5ba2-8b5f-9b5247115e68" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356384 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356385 13194139535587 true] [17592186356384 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356387 13194139535587 true] [17592186356384 1089 "q-344720" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356384 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535587 true] [17592186356384 1100 "Use calculus to find the coordinates of the closest point to $P(5,-3)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-2,1)$ and $B(6,1).$\n\nThe coordinates of the required point are\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356384 1113 #uuid "ab65483d-ab43-55de-ae49-174136d85e59" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356384 1145 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068082/assets/a86fbc456930e18159b1745f5a71fe82bf7075f3aa6d9fd4307a71a583a37a7a.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(5,-3)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10x+6y+34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,1)$ and $B(6,1).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-6)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+(y-1)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-12x+36+(y-1)^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{(y-1)^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-12x+36+\\cancel{(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ 4x+12x & =36-4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =(2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-10(2)+6y+34 \\\\  & =4+{y}^{2}-20+6y+34 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+6y+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=2y+6,\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(-3)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=-3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $(\\,2,-3\\,).$" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356386 13194139535587 true] [17592186356385 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535587 true] [17592186356385 1041 "field-1" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356385 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356386 13194139535587 true] [17592186356385 1110 #uuid "66aa18e1-391c-54a4-97aa-fa042554cd68" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356386 1034 "2" 13194139535587 true] [1759