{y}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x+4 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-6y+9 \\\\ 6y & =-6x+10-4 \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(-x+1)^{2}-2x-4(-x+1)+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x+1-2x+4x-4+5 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(0)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=-0+1=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(0,1).$" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356396 13194139535587 true] [17592186356395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356397 13194139535587 true] [17592186356395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356398 13194139535587 true] [17592186356395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356399 13194139535587 true] [17592186356395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356400 13194139535587 true] [17592186356395 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535587 true] [17592186356395 1041 "selection" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356395 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356398 13194139535587 true] [17592186356395 1110 #uuid "997ef232-78a3-5863-88f1-f3a914866af1" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356396 1034 "(0,0)" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356396 1111 #uuid "91e6b082-6609-50ff-bc91-a8b6158d69fc" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356397 1034 "(1,-2)" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356397 1111 #uuid "da861823-2efe-568f-854f-0b9c28309