-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y+4 & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-4y+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-8x+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4y+20 \\\\ 8y & =8x-16 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(x-2)^{2}-4x-4\\cdot (x-2)+8 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-4x+4-4x-4x+8+8 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-12x+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(3)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(3,1).$" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356403 13194139535587 true] [17592186356402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356404 13194139535587 true] [17592186356402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356405 13194139535587 true] [17592186356402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356406 13194139535587 true] [17592186356402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356407 13194139535587 true] [17592186356402 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535587 true] [17592186356402 1041 "selection" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356402 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356405 13194139535587 true] [17592186356402 1110 #uuid "70b1e9b0-1a90-59ac-9a5a-9d7052933325" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356403 1034 "(3,2)" 13194139535587 true] [17592186356403 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535587 true] [17592186356403 1111 #uuid "16ee3a6c-8ccf-5f04-8697-70869c8f3270" 